<a name="Intro"></a>
<h1>Introduction</h1>
This example shows how an application that solves a sequence of linear systems with the same sparsity pattern can reuse the setup of a distributed multigrid preconditioner with `LinOpFactory::generate_reuse()`.

It solves the nonlinear diffusion equation -div((1 + u^2) grad u) = f on the unit square with a Picard iteration: every iteration assembles A(u_k) and solves A(u_k) u_{k+1} = f with a multigrid preconditioned Cg, until the nonlinear residual is small.
All A(u_k) share their sparsity pattern, so after the hierarchy has been recorded once, generate_reuse() keeps the Pgm aggregates and transfer operators and only recomputes the coarse matrices, smoothers and the coarsest solver.
The example also shows how to assemble a matrix whose entries depend on values owned by neighbouring ranks without exchanging them, by reading the matrix with `assembly_mode::communicate`.

The kept aggregates only fit the later matrices if the recorded one is representative. The first iterate u_0 = 0 gives the constant-coefficient Laplacian, which Pgm coarsens poorly, so the first iteration uses generate() and the hierarchy is recorded in the second one.

To run it with multiple processes, use `mpirun -n NUM_PROCS ./distributed-multigrid-reuse [executor] [grid_side] [max_picard_iters] [refresh_interval]`.
A refresh_interval of 1 sets up the hierarchy from scratch in every iteration, which can be used to compare the setup times and iteration counts with the default reuse.

If you are using GPU devices, please make sure that you run this example with at most as many processes as you have GPU
devices available.
